  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Private Sector-Led Workforce Development Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Kosovo):  Long-term economic growth in Kosovo requires a more competitive private sector, which requires a skilled and agile workforce.  The Private Sector-Led Workforce Development is a five-year activity that provides young people with the necessary skills to meet the needs of Kosovo&apos;s high-growth industries, in particular wood processing, ICT, energy, and agribusiness sectors. This activity will develop Kosovo&apos;s workforce, building the capacity of youth with relevant skills and catalyze stronger and more strategic relationships between the private sector, government, and academia, ensuring that curricula match market needs, policies reflect a more progressive and highly integrated education system, and scalability can thrive with a youth workforce that will raise Kosovo&apos;s economic competitiveness. This activity will build Kosovo&apos;s capacity to transform the orientation of vocational education, driven by the private sector, to advance economic growth, which aligns with CAATSA Goal 8 supporting economic resilience (20% of funding) through technical assistance designed to increase workforce readiness skills, ICS Objective 3.1 &quot;Enhanced engagement and policy enactment in trade, regional economic integration, and private sector competitiveness helps Kosovo take ownership of its own prosperity,&quot; and Objective 3.3 &quot;Kosovo&apos;s human capital enhanced through better integration of underserved communities and by equipping citizens with the skills needed for a modern economy and resilient democracy that attracts investment and reduces &quot;brain drain.&quot; It aligns with JRS Goal 4.1: &quot;Build and equip a diverse, inclusive, resilient, and dynamic workforce&quot; and Objective 2.2: &quot;Support inclusive and sustainable economic growth and opportunity for communities around the globe&quot; by giving youth opportunities to fully participate in the economy and in Kosovo&apos;s democracy... ..</narrative>
  </description>
